WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.250
Good morning John, and happy Pizzamas.

00:00:01.250 --> 00:00:04.640
As I think you expected, people have been
enjoying your green-screen time.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.450
They're calling it John Greenscreened; it's
a thing, it's beautiful.

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:12.120
But I want to skip back another video, to
the almost incalculably distant Friday video

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:14.970
that I did, in which I used the word "dipthong".

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:16.881
Which, unfortunately, does not exist.

00:00:16.881 --> 00:00:22.369
The word I was going for was diphthong, which
is also really great.

00:00:22.369 --> 00:00:27.780
I have pronounced and spelled diphthong wrong
for at least a decade.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:33.019
I am not embarrassed about my misunderstanding
with the diphthong word, and I want to know

00:00:33.019 --> 00:00:34.019
why!

00:00:34.019 --> 00:00:37.780
In college, I did an oral presentation on
Leon Battista Alberti, who was a mathematician,

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:40.860
architect, and artist of the Renaissance.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.681
Super cool dude, if you ever want to learn
about him.

00:00:42.681 --> 00:00:46.519
He designed a great number of the fronts of
buildings in Florence.

00:00:46.519 --> 00:00:51.769
Those fronts of buildings are called that
word (façade), which I said, for the entire

00:00:51.769 --> 00:00:53.350
presentation, "fah-caid".

00:00:53.350 --> 00:01:00.420
Like, 26 times, I said "fah-caid", and I knew
something wasn't going well, but I just powered

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:07.049
through, you know, and then found out later,
from a friend, "fuh-SAHD".

00:01:07.049 --> 00:01:08.049
Anhhhhhhh . . .

00:01:08.049 --> 00:01:13.509
So I mispronounced Diphthong in front of 200,000
people - didn't bother me.

00:01:13.509 --> 00:01:18.090
"Fah-caid", in front of twenty people, left
a permanent scar.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.340
Like, I still have a hard time telling that
story.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.630
What is the difference between those two things?

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:24.229
I think it's mostly confidence.

00:01:24.229 --> 00:01:29.979
Like, being confident when you're wrong is
one of the most amazing things you can be.

00:01:29.979 --> 00:01:33.030
It's wonderful... if you can pull it off,
which is impossible.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:37.320
I don't mean confidence in believing a thing
that is wrong and continuing to believe a

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:40.649
thing despite knowing that it's wrong, which
some people are really into.

00:01:40.649 --> 00:01:44.359
I mean, finding out you're wrong, knowing
you're wrong, and being confident in your

00:01:44.359 --> 00:01:45.900
understanding of your own wrongness.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:50.859
When you see someone be super-wrong and be
like, "Eh, whatever, yeah, I was wrong.

00:01:50.859 --> 00:01:51.859
Yeah!

00:01:51.859 --> 00:01:52.859
Thanks for letting me know!"

00:01:52.859 --> 00:01:53.999
you're like "That person is cool.

00:01:53.999 --> 00:01:55.109
They're cool.

00:01:55.109 --> 00:01:56.340
How do I get that?"

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:00.219
Feeling fine about being wrong is the logical
way to feel, because, as I have pointed out

00:02:00.219 --> 00:02:05.430
in previous videos, I am a towering mountain
of ignorance, and all simplicity is a lie.

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:07.409
Even when you're right, you're a little bit
wrong!

00:02:07.409 --> 00:02:11.200
Getting to a place where I feel comfortable
enough with myself to have internalized that

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:14.930
truth, even a little bit, has been a lifelong
process.

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:20.480
And really, it only works when I'm in places
and with people who I trust and know and understand

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:24.170
and feel good about and feel confident around,
which is the case here - the Vlogbrothers

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.030
channel and nerdfighteria.

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:30.460
Embarrassment really isn't about being wrong,
it's about feeling wrong, and it is hard for

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:34.540
me to feel wrong here, because you guys are
nice.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:39.860
Sometimes the most embarrassing part of the
embarrassment is the embarrassment itself!

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:40.860
That's weird!

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:44.740
In the comments on my Friday video, I owned
up, and I said that I mispronounced "dipthong,"

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:48.650
just like John recently mispronounced "hectacre,"
and I asked people to tell me their hectacre

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:49.650
moments.

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:52.940
And I read through them, just before I made
this video, and it was wonderful.

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:58.830
There's like 300 replies and they're all hilarious
and wince-inducing.

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:02.480
But several of them were also façade, which
made me feel better about myself, and I think

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:06.180
in general, that thread was cathartic for
all the people who participated in it.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:09.250
Being wrong is part of what it is to be a
person.

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:12.200
And you have to work on how to be wrong without
feeling wrong.

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:13.660
I think that thread was a good start.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:17.401
So I'm going to ask all of you: if you got
a little time, tell us an embarrassing thing

00:03:17.401 --> 00:03:18.920
that has happened to you.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:23.550
We can read it and feel better about ourselves,
knowing that everyone does dumb things!

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:26.560
But I think owning it is the number one thing
you can do, and I think the other thing you

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:31.740
can do is let people know who are admitting
to their embarrassing things that have happened

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:37.090
to them, that they're awesome, and that that's
hilarious, and not to worry about it.

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:41.440
That's hopefully what's going on in the comments;
John, I'll see you tomorrow.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:42.440
Hi, endscreen!

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:47.220
This is sad - only three days left of Pizzamas,
but the Project for Awesome is coming up,

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:50.270
so there are reasons to celebrate the future.

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:53.470
Because of Paper Towns shoot dates, we're
actually moving the Project for Awesome up

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:56.390
this year, it's going to be the 12th and 13th
of December.

00:03:56.390 --> 00:03:59.000
So mark your calendars and start planning
your videos.

